AUDIO: 'This party is insane. &gt;&gt; You say that like it\'s a bad thing. &gt;&gt; You\'re smiling. I win. &gt;&gt; Don\'t get used to it. &gt;&gt; This is a bad idea. &gt;&gt; What if I told you that one single image'

[00:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can contain everything for a full AI video scene? The characters, the outfits, the props, the background, the color palette, the mood, and even the story direction. In this video, I will show you how to'

[00:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'take one simple ingredients collage and turn it into a cinematic animated scene in LTX 2.3 directly inside ComfyUI. So, if you want to learn how to generate video when your only input is one'

[00:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'collage with all the visual ingredients, this is exactly what we are going to do today. So, hello everyone. Before we start, please subscribe to the channel, leave a like, and write a comment under the video. Tell me what kind of scene'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you would like to create with this method. Cyberpunk, fantasy, product video, animation, short film, or something completely different. It really helps the channel, and it also helps me understand what tutorials you want to see next. Today, we are going to'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look at LTX 2.3 ingredients IC Laura. And honestly, this is one of the most interesting ways to work with image to video generation because here the input image is not just a starting frame. It'

[01:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is more like a box of visual materials. We give the model a collage with everything we want to see in the final scene, and then we guide it with the prompt so it builds one coherent cinematic shot. So, the idea of this'

[01:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video is very simple. We take one image where we have all the important ingredients, our characters, their clothes, shoes, accessories, props, the environment, the city background, the lighting style, and the color'

[01:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'references. Then, we load this image into the workflow, write the correct prompt, add the correct negative prompt, and generate a video scene that uses those elements naturally. And this is the most important point. We do not want'

[02:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'LTX to generate the collage itself. We do not want a mood board. We do not want a grid. We do not want separate panels on a white background. We want a normal scene as if these ingredients were placed into one animated film shot.'

[02:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'First, let\'s look at the input image. As you can see, this is not a normal cinematic frame. This is a collage. Here I have two stylized 3D girls, their full body references, close-up face'

[02:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'references, outfits, shoes, accessories, a glowing holographic tablet, rooftop props, cables, metal boxes, city background elements, drones, and also a purple blue color palette. So, visually,'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this image contains almost everything the model needs to understand the scene. But, the image itself is not the final composition. It is only the source of the ingredients. That is why we need to be very careful'

[02:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with how we explain the task to the model. If we simply load this image and write a weak prompt, the model can easily misunderstand what we want. It can keep the white background. It can create a split-screen result. It can'

[03:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duplicate the characters. Or, it can make something that still looks like a reference sheet. So, my first rule for this workflow is this: Never describe the collage as a collage. Do not write, "Use this reference sheet." Do not'

[03:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write, "Copy this mood board." Do not write, "Generate from this grid." Instead, describe the final video scene. Describe what should exist in the final shot, where the characters are, what they are doing, what objects are around'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them, what the camera is doing, and what the lighting should feel like. For example, in this case, I describe two young hacker girls on a dark cyberpunk rooftop at night. One girl is holding an ice drink. The other girl is holding a'

[03:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'glowing purple holographic tablet. Around them, there are industrial rooftop cables, metal equipment, warning panels, antennas, and small drones flying in the distance. Behind them, there is a neon city skyline with purple'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and blue lighting. This is much better because now I am not asking the model to reproduce the collage. I am directing a scene. Now, let\'s move to the workflow in ComfyUI. Here, I already have the LTX 2.3 ingredients workflow prepared. At'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the top, we have the model loading section. In my case, I use the LTX 2.3 base model, then the distilled Lora, and then the ingredients I see Lora. The exact file names can depend on your'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setup, but the logic is the same. The base model gives us the video generation foundation. The distilled Lora helps with the generation setup, and the ingredients I see Lora helps the model interpret our input image as a'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'collection of usable visual elements. And by the way, you do not need to rebuild all of this manually from zero. I will leave the workflow in the description under this video. The link will take you to my Patreon, where you'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can download the workflow for free. Inside, you will also find links to all the important elements, the models, Lora\'s, nodes, and everything needed to reproduce this setup correctly. So, if you want to follow the tutorial'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'step-by-step, just check the description below the video. This is the key difference from a simple image-to-video workflow. In a normal image-to-video setup, the model often tries to continue the input image as directly as possible,'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but here, with ingredients I see Lora, the model can use the image more like visual guidance. It can take the character identity, the clothing, the props, the environment, and the atmosphere, and then assemble them into'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a new cinematic frame. So, here, in the load image node, I load my collage. This is the main input for the scene, and before I even touch the prompt, I always check if the collage is readable.'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The characters should not be too small. Important props should be visible. Clothing details should be clear. If the necklace, shoes, tablet, or drone are important, they should not be hidden somewhere in the corner. The model'

[05:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cannot use details that it cannot clearly understand. Also, I try not to make the collage too chaotic. Yes, we can add many ingredients, but if everything overlaps or if the image is too messy, the model may start mixing'

[06:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'objects together. So, I usually keep the main characters large, props around them, background references to the side, and color references somewhere clean and simple. It does not need to be beautiful as a design. It needs to be'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'understandable for the model. Now, let\'s talk about the prompt, because this is where many people make the biggest mistake. They think the image is enough, but for this workflow, the image and the prompt must work together.'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The image gives the model visual memory. The prompt gives the model direction. Without the prompt, the model may know what the ingredients are, but it may not know how to combine them. I usually write the prompt in English. You can'

[06:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'think through the idea in any language, but for LTX, I prefer a clear English cinematic prompt. If needed, I can write the idea in Ukrainian, translate it with DeepL, or ask ChatGPT to clean it up and turn it into a proper LTX prompt. The'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'main thing is that the final prompt should describe the finished shot, not the reference image. Also, on my Patreon, I will include special AI agent instructions for writing prompts specifically for LTX ingredients Laura'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'video generation. The idea is very simple. You give the agent your collage image, explain what kind of scene you want, and the agent helps you write a clean, strong prompt for this exact workflow. So, instead of guessing how to'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'describe the scene, you can use a ready prompt writing system designed for ingredients Laura. A good prompt for this kind of workflow usually has several parts. First, I describe the main subject. Then, I describe the'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'visual ingredients that must appear in the scene. Then, I describe the action. After that, I describe the camera, the lighting, the atmosphere, and the final visual style. So, it is not just a random list of words. It is more like a small scene direction. For example, the'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'subject is two stylized 3D girls. The ingredients are the cyberpunk outfits, the ice drink, the holographic tablet, the rooftop equipment, the drones, and the neon city. The action is that they'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are standing on a rooftop and analyzing a glowing holographic interface. The camera can slowly push in. The lighting is purple and blue, soft but cinematic. The style is stylized animated film, high detail, clean motion, and coherent'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'composition. And now the negative prompt. This part is very important here. In many workflows, people use negative prompts only for things like ugly face, bad hands, blurry image, low quality, and so on. But for ingredients'

[08:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I see Laura, I also use the negative prompt to block the collage format itself. So, I add terms like collage, mood board, grid layout, split screen, multiple panels, reference sheet, white'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background layout, duplicated character, wrong face, changed identity, wrong outfit, missing accessories, incorrect props, extra limbs, warped hands, broken anatomy, floating objects, inconsistent'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scale, flickering, blurry face, bad motion, jump cuts, chaotic camera movement, and distorted background. Why do I do that? Because the model sees the input collage. It sees that the image'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contains many separate parts. So, if I do not clearly tell it what to avoid, it may preserve that structure in the final video. The negative prompt helps push the model away from the reference board'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'look and toward one clean scene. After that, I check the prepare condition section. This is where the workflow prepares the input image for video generation. Here we have nodes for resizing the image, creating the video'

[09:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conditioning, repeating the image batch, and setting the video length. In this workflow, I use a normal video setup with 8-bit depth, 25 frames per second, and a resize method like Lanczos. The'

[09:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'resize value is important. If I resize the collage too small, the model may lose details, the accessories, the shoe design, the tablet shape, small props, or background clues. But, if I make the'

[09:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'input too large, generation becomes heavier and can require more VRAM. So, I usually start with a reasonable value and only increase it if I see that important ingredients are missing in the output. The same applies to video'

[10:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'length. If I want a short test, I do not need a very long generation. But, if I want a more complete scene, I can increase the frame count. In this setup, the workflow is designed for a normal cinematic video fragment, not just a'

[10:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'single moving image. But, when testing a new prompt or a new collage, I always recommend starting with a shorter generation first. It saves time, and it helps you understand if the direction is correct. In the sampler section, I keep'

[10:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the setups stable while testing. For example, I can keep the seed the same while I adjust only the prompt or the collage. This is useful because if the seed changes every time, it becomes harder to understand what actually'

[10:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'improved the result. When the seed stays the same, I can compare the generations more honestly. Now, I run the generation. On the left, we can see the input collage. On the right, we can preview the generated video. And this is'

[10:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the interesting moment because now we can see if the workflow understood the task correctly. The goal is not just to make something beautiful. The goal is to see whether the model actually used the ingredients from the collage and place'

[11:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'them into one believable scene. And here is the result. In my opinion, this is exactly where ingredients I see Laura becomes useful. So, if you are interested in ComfyUI, LTX, local AI video generation, and AI'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'filmmaking, subscribe to the channel. Leave a like if this tutorial was useful, and write in the comments what kind of ingredients collage you want to try next. And also check the description below the video. There you will find the'

[11:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'link to my Patreon, where you can download this workflow for free with links to the needed models, LoRAs, nodes, and useful materials. On Patreon, I will also share the AI agent'

[11:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'instructions for this workflow. One agent for writing better prompts for LTX ingredients LoRA video, and another agent for creating a proper ingredients collage from one reference image. So, if you want faster, cleaner, and more'

[12:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'controlled results, subscribe to my Patreon and use these tools together with the workflow. Thank you for watching, and I will see you in the next one.'
